{"records":[{"id":"wjdntc39sp","title":"Genome assembly, quality-control, annotation, and phylogenomic data for Pediococcus acidilactici strain AP1 isolated from common carp","description":"Description\nThis dataset contains quality-control reports, genome assembly files, structural and functional genome annotations, CRISPR-Cas analysis results, and genome-based phylogenetic data for Pediococcus acidilactici strain AP1. Strain AP1 was isolated from the intestinal tract of a healthy common carp (Cyprinus carpio) collected from a fish farm in the Tashkent region, Uzbekistan. Whole-genome sequencing was performed using the Illumina MiSeq i100 Plus platform with paired-end sequencing. Reads were quality assessed using FastQC and filtered using fastp. De novo assembly was performed using SPAdes. Assembly quality was evaluated using QUAST, BUSCO, and\nCheckM2. Structural and functional annotation was performed using PGAP, Prokka, RAST, eggNOG-mapper, and InterProScan. CRISPR-Cas loci were evaluated using CRISPRCasFinder, and genome-based phylogenetic analysis was performed using TYGS.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-11T00:06:05.284Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wjdntc39sp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wjdntc39sp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Murodali","last_name":"Boboqulov","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fazliddin","last_name":"Kobilov","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genome Annotation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jbpx7jptnn","title":"Genome assembly, quality-control, annotation, and phylogenomic data for Pediococcus acidilactici strain AP1 isolated from common carp","description":"This dataset contains quality-control reports, genome assembly files, structural and functional genome annotations, CRISPR-Cas analysis results, and genome-based phylogenetic data for Pediococcus acidilactici strain AP1. Strain AP1 was isolated from the intestinal tract of a healthy common carp (Cyprinus carpio) collected from a fish farm in the Tashkent region, Uzbekistan. Whole-genome sequencing was performed using the Illumina MiSeq i100 Plus platform with paired-end sequencing. Reads were quality assessed using FastQC and filtered using fastp. De novo assembly was performed using SPAdes. Assembly quality was evaluated using QUAST, BUSCO, and\nCheckM2. Structural and functional annotation was performed using PGAP, Prokka, RAST, eggNOG-mapper, and InterProScan. CRISPR-Cas loci were evaluated using CRISPRCasFinder, and genome-based phylogenetic analysis was performed using TYGS.\n\nDATA ACCESSIONS\nBioProject: PRJNA1476265\nBioSample: SAMN60696776\nSRA: SRR39936145\nAssembly: GCA_058312935.1\nWGS: JBZEGU000000000","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-03T09:42:09.068Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jbpx7jptnn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jbpx7jptnn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fazliddin","last_name":"Kobilov","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Murodali","last_name":"Bobokulov","orcid":"0000-0001-7222-5661"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genome Annotation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"835fcfbvhs","title":"Preparation of CRISPR/Cas9 and gRNA plasmids","description":"Human genome engineering has been transformed by the introduction of the CRISPR (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats)/Cas (CRISPR-associated) system found in most bacteria and archaea. Type II CRISPR/Cas systems have been engineered to induce RNA-guided genome editing in human cells, where small RNAs function together with Cas9 nucleases for sequence-specific cleavage of target sequences. Here we describe the protocol for Cas9-mediated human genome engineering, including construct building and transfection methods necessary for delivering Cas9 and guide RNA (gRNA) into human-induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and HEK293 cells. Following genome editing, we also describe methods to assess genome editing effi- ciency using next-generation sequencing and isolate monoclonal hiPSCs with the desired modifications for downstream applications. Keywords: genome engineering • CRISPR • human stem cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2016-12-12T12:58:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/835fcfbvhs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/835fcfbvhs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Church","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Method"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9pd453pm7k","title":"Long RNA-Sequencing Analysis of Extracellular Vesicles secreted by colorectal cancer cells","description":"Although much is known about extracellular miRNA, the identity, regulation, and functional roles of secreted coding and long noncoding RNAs (lncRNAs, &gt;200nt) are largely unknown. We have previously shown that mutant KRAS colorectal cancer (CRC) cells release EVs containing distinct proteomes, miRNAs, and circular RNAs. Here, we comprehensively identify the broad and diverse classes of CRC extracellular long RNAs secreted in EVs and demonstrate differential export of specific RNAs. We show that distinct noncoding RNAs including antisense transcripts and transcripts derived from pseudogenes are enriched in EVs compared to cellular profiles. Raw data from sequencing and analysis can be found at the supplied link (including the FASTQ data files). ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-09-04T16:46:14.504Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9pd453pm7k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9pd453pm7k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Hinger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qi","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"James","last_name":"Patton","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing","Gene Ontology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sxrvzkr4hh","title":"Pooled in vitro and in vivo CRISPR-Cas9 screening identifies tumor suppressors in human colon organoids","description":"UMI validation screen, original data from in vivo tumor suppressor CRISPR/Cas9 screening experiment. Figure 6.\nExperimental setup: 281 tumor suppressor gRNAs, positive controls, neutral controls and gRNAs targeting essential genes were introduced into the CRISPR-UMI lentivirus (Michlits et al., 2017). Transduced AK organoids (APC-KO and KRASG12D) were transplanted subcutaneously in NSG mice followed by barcode sequencing of the tumor pool (from 10 mice) and 5 individual tumors. Clone and read number were determined by next generation sequencing. The conventional gRNA enrichment analysis was compared to UMI-CRISPR-screening with outlier removal.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-20T15:46:17.868Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sxrvzkr4hh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sxrvzkr4hh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Henner","last_name":"Farin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cszpw4c66t","title":"Structural insights into Cas9-mediated prespacer selection in CRISPR-Cas adaptation. Gaizauskaite et al.","description":"During CRISPR-Cas adaptation, prokaryotic cells become immunized by the insertion of foreign DNA fragments, termed spacers, into the host genome to serve as templates for RNA-guided immunity. Spacer acquisition relies on the Cas1-Cas2 integrase and accessory proteins, which select DNA sequences flanked by the protospacer adjacent motif (PAM) and insert them into the CRISPR array. It has been shown that in type II-A systems selection of PAM-proximal prespacers is mediated by the effector nuclease Cas9, which forms a ‘supercomplex’ with the Cas1-Cas2 integrase and the Csn2 protein. Here, we present cryo-electron microscopy structures of the type II-A prespacer selection supercomplex in the DNA-scanning and two distinct PAM-bound configurations, providing insights into the mechanism of Cas9-mediated prespacer selection in type II-A CRISPR-Cas systems. Repurposing of Cas9 by the CRISPR adaptation machinery for prespacer selection characterized here demonstrates Cas9 plasticity and expands our knowledge of the Cas9 biology.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-02-20T09:45:34.481Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cszpw4c66t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cszpw4c66t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ugne","last_name":"Gaizauskaite","orcid":"0009-0008-9137-5428"},{"first_name":"Giedre","last_name":"Tamulaitiene","orcid":"0000-0002-9794-3544"},{"first_name":"Arunas","last_name":"Silanskas","orcid":"0000-0002-6385-6928"},{"first_name":"Giedrius","last_name":"Gasiunas","orcid":"0000-0003-2509-9054"},{"first_name":"Virginijus","last_name":"Siksnys","orcid":"0000-0003-1983-2096"},{"first_name":"Giedrius","last_name":"Sasnauskas","orcid":"0000-0002-3185-2682"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/02gs16m83","name":"Lietuvos Mokslo Taryba","grant_id":"S-MIP-19-32"}],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vt434wb4b4","title":"Anti-CRISPR phages cooperate to overcome CRISPR-Cas immunity","description":"Some phages encode anti-CRISPR (acr) genes, which antagonize bacterial CRISPR-Cas immune systems by binding components of its machinery, but it is less clear how deployment of these acr genes impacts phage replication and epidemiology. Here we demonstrate that bacteria with CRISPR-Cas resistance are still partially immune to Acr-encoding phage. As a consequence, Acr-phages often need to cooperate in order to overcome CRISPR resistance, with a first phage taking down the host CRISPR-Cas immune system to allow a second Acr-phage to successfully replicate. This cooperation leads to epidemiological tipping points in which the initial density of Acr-phage tips the balance from phage extinction to a phage epidemic. Furthermore, both higher levels of CRISPR-Cas immunity and weaker Acr activities shift the tipping points towards higher phage densities. Collectively these data help to understand how interactions between phage-encoded immune suppressors and the CRISPR systems they target shape bacteria-phage population dynamics.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-05-22T22:30:09.605Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vt434wb4b4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vt434wb4b4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mariann","last_name":"Landsberger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sylvain","last_name":"Gandon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sean","last_name":"Meaden","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Clare","last_name":"Rollie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hélène","last_name":"Chabas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anne","last_name":"Chevallereau","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Angus","last_name":"Buckling","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Edze R.","last_name":"Westra","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stineke","last_name":"van Houte","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Epidemiology","Biological Sciences","Bacteria","Bacteriophage","Immunosuppression","Resistance (Fungal Pathogenesis)","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"295rz7dxr9","title":"Recent Progress and Prospects of CRISPR-Cas12a-Based Detection Technologies in Swine Viral Diseases","description":"Figure 1. Trends in CRISPR-Based Diagnostic Research from 2017 to 2025\nFigure 2. Classification of CRISPR/Cas Systems\nFigure 3. CRISPR/Cas Immune Mechanism\nFigure 4. Domain organization and crystal structure of FnCpf1\nDomain architecture of FnCpf1; (B) Crystal structure of FnCpf1 (PDB: 6GTC)\nFigure 5. Schematic illustration of nucleic acid amplification techniques coupled with the CRISPR-Cas12a detection principle","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-16T22:14:41.005Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/295rz7dxr9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/295rz7dxr9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiaona","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","CRISPR-Cas12A"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pbzg77hv4n","title":"COL7A1 Ex73 Frameshift NGS data","description":"NGS data for primary WT as wells as primary and immortalized RDEB keratinocytes treated with frameshift CRISPR gRNAs. gRNA with precise and predictable repair outcome (+1 nucleotide insertion).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-01-07T09:21:52.382Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pbzg77hv4n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pbzg77hv4n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Johannes","last_name":"Bischof","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"d5vw9sjcrv","title":"A target expression threshold dictates invader defense  and autoimmunity by CRISPR-Cas13","description":"Immune systems must recognize and clear foreign invaders without eliciting autoimmunity. CRISPR-Cas immune systems in prokaryotes manage this task by following two criteria: extensive guide:target complementarity and a defined target-flanking motif. Here we report an additional requirement for RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas13 systems: expression of the target transcript exceeding a threshold. This finding is based on targeting endogenous non-essential transcripts, which rarely elicited dormancy through collateral RNA degradation. Instead, eliciting dormancy required over-expressing targeted transcripts above a threshold. A genome-wide screen confirmed target expression levels as the principal determinant of cytotoxic autoimmunity and revealed that the threshold shifts with the guide:target pair. This expression threshold ensured defense against a lytic bacteriophage yet allowed tolerance of a targeted beneficial gene expressed from an invading plasmid. These findings establish target expression levels as a third criterion for immune defense by RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas systems, buffering against autoimmunity and distinguishing pathogenic and benign invaders.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-08T11:55:48.983Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/d5vw9sjcrv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/d5vw9sjcrv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elena","last_name":"Vialetto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanying","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Collins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katharina","last_name":"Wandera","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lars","last_name":"Barquist","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chase","last_name":"Beisel","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis","Data Access"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":142319,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":171,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":142319,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":141482,"restricted":72,"embargoed":765},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2355},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":712},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":483},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":438},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":428},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":415},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":407},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":339},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":285},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":236}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":139645},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1915},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":187},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":142},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":42},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":28}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":543},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":411},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":394},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":315}]}}